[
    {
        "content": "<p>I am working with <code>iSup</code>s of <code>primaryComponent</code>s and the <code>[Fact p.Prime]</code> is making it fail to elaborate. For example, I am unable to write <code> ⨆ p : (Nat.card G).primeFactors, primaryComponent G p.val</code> or <code>∏ p ∈ S, Nat.card (primaryComponent G p.val)</code> without the following:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">failed</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">to</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">synthesize</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">of</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">class</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Fact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Prime</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>My current workaround is to bundle it into a def with <code>haveI : Fact p.val.Prime := ...</code>.</p>\n<p>Is there a reason <code>primaryComponent</code> isn't total over <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\N</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">N</span></span></span></span>? <code>IsPGroup</code> and <code>Sylow</code> are both total and much easier to work with. Happy to PR a fix if it is warranted.</p>",
        "id": 595497931,
        "sender_full_name": "Richard Osborn",
        "timestamp": 1778945039
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Can you provide a <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mwe.html\">#mwe</a> ?</p>",
        "id": 595518267,
        "sender_full_name": "Filippo A. E. Nuccio",
        "timestamp": 1778958317
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>There are several <code>primaryComponent</code>s, this is why a <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mwe.html\">#mwe</a> is always useful. But if you mean <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=CommMonoid.primaryComponent#doc\">docs#CommMonoid.primaryComponent</a> , it does look like the assumption can just be removed and that this would probably be a good idea. Try making a PR removing it and see what happens?</p>",
        "id": 595519077,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1778958770
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh I see, the issue is that with the definition given this would not be a subgroup. You'd have to ask that it divided p^n and now suddenly this is more annoying to use. Try changing it to divides, make some helper lemmas that apply when the Fact is present and see if you can get mathlib compiling again and how much more horrible it has become.</p>",
        "id": 595519206,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1778958864
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sorry, here's an MWE. I want a family of primary components indexed by <code>(Nat.card G).primeFactors</code>, to plug into <code>iSup</code>, <code>Finset.prod</code>, <code>iSupIndep</code> etc. I'd like to state an internal direct product decomposition of finite abelian groups.<br>\nMWE:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">GroupTheory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Torsion</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- This fails to elaborate:</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Finite</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">card</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">primeFactors</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Subgroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CommGroup</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">primaryComponent</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">val</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- failed to synthesize instance of type class Fact (Nat.Prime ↑p)</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Current workaround: wrap in a def</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">primaryComponentFamily</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">card</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">primeFactors</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Subgroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">haveI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">val</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Prime</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">prime_of_mem_primeFactors</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">property</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">CommGroup</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">primaryComponent</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">val</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 595521125,
        "sender_full_name": "Richard Osborn",
        "timestamp": 1778960109
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/.60.5BFact.20p.2EPrime.5D.60.20in.20.60CommGroup.2EprimaryComponent.60/near/595519206\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Oh I see, the issue is that with the definition given this would not be a subgroup. You'd have to ask that it divided p^n and now suddenly this is more annoying to use. Try changing it to divides, make some helper lemmas that apply when the Fact is present and see if you can get mathlib compiling again and how much more horrible it has become.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes, I think this would match how <code>PGroup</code> is defined.</p>",
        "id": 595521194,
        "sender_full_name": "Richard Osborn",
        "timestamp": 1778960150
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You mean <code>IsPGroup</code>? Well the difference is that this is a predicate on groups, so it doesn't matter if it's a junk predicate for non-primes. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=CommGroup.primaryComponent#doc\">docs#CommGroup.primaryComponent</a> is data (a subgroup) so it has to actually return a subgroup given a junk value. This is why I'm suggesting this refactor of <code>Submonoid.primaryComponent</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">primaryComponent</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Submonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">carrier</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">orderOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∣</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">one_mem'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">pow_zero</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">orderOf_one</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">mul_mem'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hg₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hg₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"n\">fix</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">this</span><span class=\"bp\">&gt;</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and then prove that under <code>[Fact p.Prime]</code>, <code>lemma mem_primaryComponent: g \\in G.primaryComponent \\iff ∃ n : ℕ, orderOf g = p ^ n</code>, and you'll probably then have to rewrite <code>mem_primaryComponent</code> in some places.</p>",
        "id": 595534624,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1778969455
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>can that be written as <code>∃ n : ℕ, g ^ (p ^ n) = 1</code></p>",
        "id": 595535352,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1778969861
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Just wrote a PR. Seems relatively clean. Seen here <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/39484\">#39484</a></p>",
        "id": 595535434,
        "sender_full_name": "Richard Osborn",
        "timestamp": 1778969898
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"816344\">Aaron Liu</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/.60.5BFact.20p.2EPrime.5D.60.20in.20.60CommGroup.2EprimaryComponent.60/near/595535352\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>can that be written as <code>∃ n : ℕ, g ^ (p ^ n) = 1</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Oh yeah I guess! (in fact this is what's done in IsPGroup)</p>",
        "id": 595538858,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1778972091
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>My idea is to introduce <code>S</code>-primary component, i.e. contains elements <code>g</code> whose <code>↑(orderOf g).primeFactors ⊆ S</code>. But looks like <code>∃ n : ℕ, g ^ (p ^ n) = 1</code> is more brilliant.</p>",
        "id": 595618837,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1779026440
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(similar to <a href=\"https://github.com/acmepjz/lean-iwasawa/blob/f32bf20d9bb3ae2314e0371bbe12436f11b4e4cf/Iwasawalib/FieldTheory/IntermediateField/MaximalGaloisSExtension.lean#L92\">https://github.com/acmepjz/lean-iwasawa/blob/f32bf20d9bb3ae2314e0371bbe12436f11b4e4cf/Iwasawalib/FieldTheory/IntermediateField/MaximalGaloisSExtension.lean#L92</a>)</p>",
        "id": 595619013,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1779026535
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"366779\">Jz Pan</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/.60.5BFact.20p.2EPrime.5D.60.20in.20.60CommGroup.2EprimaryComponent.60/near/595618837\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>My idea is to introduce <code>S</code>-primary component, i.e. contains elements <code>g</code> whose <code>↑(orderOf g).primeFactors ⊆ S</code>. But looks like <code>∃ n : ℕ, g ^ (p ^ n) = 1</code> is more brilliant.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The <code>S</code>-primary component only forms a subgroup for abelian groups. This is exactly the Hall <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">π</span></span></span></span>-subgroup.</p>",
        "id": 595621949,
        "sender_full_name": "Richard Osborn",
        "timestamp": 1779028243
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But <code>CommGroup.primaryComponent</code> also require the group being abelian. Otherwise it is not a subgroup.</p>",
        "id": 595748150,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1779092504
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Could you just take the subgroup generated by those elements? (This is not so interesting outside of the abelian case, but this will give you a subgroup.)</p>",
        "id": 595934177,
        "sender_full_name": "Antoine Chambert-Loir",
        "timestamp": 1779137536
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you are extending to the noncommutative case, then probably some thought should be given to which generalization would serve best (e.g., the p-core would be another option).</p>",
        "id": 595948410,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1779143581
    }
]